## 2. Architecture Diagram

High-level flow (real v2.1 flow — no LLM, no n8n, no payment webhook):

1. Buyer browses the storefront; product/blog data is hydrated from Payload via
   Next.js server components (`getPayload({ config })`), served from the ISR
   cache. Product/blog detail pages revalidate on Payload `afterChange` hook.
2. Buyer clicks **Contactar por WhatsApp** on a product card → `wa.me` deep link
   opens WhatsApp to Shirley's business number. No system involvement beyond the
   storefront link. (PRD §3.1 ct-text, AC-01.2)
3. Shirley receives the message on her phone. She IS the context layer of the
   MVP: she bridges social channels → system by typing `/nuevo` in Telegram.
4. The Telegram update hits `POST /api/webhooks/telegram` (Next.js route
   handler). The handler validates `X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token` and the
   authorized chat ID, then dispatches the message to `packages/telegram`.
   (PRD §3.4.5, AC-04.12 / AC-04.13)
5. The command handler reads/mutates Payload (`orders`, `products`) and replies
   via the Telegram Bot API (`sendMessage`).
6. Order state mutations (`CHECKOUT_READY → PAID → DISPATCHED`) happen ONLY via
   admin buttons or Telegram commands. There is no event bus, no n8n flow, no
   dispatch-sheet auto-generation. (PRD §3.3, PRD §3.4.1)
7. A 09:00 America/Bogota external cron calls
   `POST /api/cron/daily-digest`, which queries Payload for yesterday's
   activity and pushes the digest message via the Telegram Bot API.
   (PRD §3.4.3, AC-04.10)

