## 5. Security Considerations

- **No LLM attack surface:** there is no model invocation in the runtime path,
  so prompt-injection, token-abuse, and cost-blowup risks do not exist for v2.1.
  (They return — with explicit mitigations — in the deferred agentic phase.)
- **Telegram webhook authentication:** `POST /api/webhooks/telegram` validates
  the `X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token` header against `TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET`
  using a constant-time compare and rejects invalid/missing secrets with 401
  before any DB access. (PRD §3.4.5, AC-04.12.)
- **Telegram authorization:** only Shirley's chat ID
  (`SHIRLEY_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID`) is authorized. Messages from any other chat ID
  receive a polite "No autorizado" and no state mutation occurs. (AC-04.13.)
- **Input validation:** every command that accepts `AX-XXXX` validates the
  format via `^AX-[A-Z2-9]{4}$` before touching the DB. `/nuevo` parses with
  tolerance (separators `— - : |`; strips `$ . , COP`; trims whitespace) and
  on failure returns a format example without creating an order. (PRD §3.4.1,
  AC-04.2, AC-04.3.)
- **Idempotency:** `/pagado` on an already-`PAID` order and `/despachado` on
  an already-`DISPATCHED` order return the "Ya está confirmado ✅" / "Ya está
  despachado 📦" message without mutating state. (PRD §3.4.1, AC-04.4.)
- **No public writes:** no public REST/GraphQL mutation surface on `orders` or
  `products`. The storefront is read-only (ISR); only the authenticated admin
  and the validated Telegram webhook can mutate. (PRD §4 — Security.)
- **Order snapshot immutability:** `orders.items` freezes product name + price
  at creation time. Editing a product later never rewrites existing orders.
  (AC-03.5.)
- **Admin auth:** Payload admin is protected by `users` collection auth
  (`auth: true`). MVP uses `read: () => true` as a known limitation; formal
  roles are deferred and tracked in the BRD/PRD. (PRD §3.3.)
- **Secrets:** `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN`, `TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET`,
  `SHIRLEY_TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID`, `WHATSAPP_BUSINESS_NUMBER`, Supabase keys, and
  cron signing keys are environment-managed, never committed. (PRD §4.)

